WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.004 --> 00:00:03.030
This light left a quasar long ago.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.696
The strip below is this quasar's light, as a perfect telescope riding with it would record it. For now, all of it gets through.

3
00:00:04.746 --> 00:00:05.988
Something stood in its way.

4
00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:09.538
Galaxies sit inside gas too faint to see.

5
00:00:10.007 --> 00:00:11.770
The gas shows itself only by the light it takes from things behind it. These colours are our choice, not its light.

6
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.550
We ride this one beam through it. Every beam meets different gas, so every spectrum differs. Pick another beam at any time.

7
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.600
density: where the gas is

8
00:00:18.992 --> 00:00:21.360
Every cell has a density, heat and speed. The beam touches hundreds of gas cells. Each one lights up as the light reaches it.

9
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.553
So far, almost nothing is taken. By now O VI has taken 1.1 % of the light at its deepest colour.

10
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:27.550
Each cell leaves a shadow. Atoms take a sliver of one colour out of the light: the first cell's dip was 5.6 % deep. The rest goes on.

11
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.600
heat: which gas is warm

12
00:00:31.228 --> 00:00:35.780
A cool clump.

13
00:00:36.138 --> 00:00:39.110
One hydrogen atom in ten keeps its electron. That is at its core. In warm-hot gas only one in 200 000 does: about 20 000 times fewer. Neutral hydrogen takes the colour we follow.

14
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.550
One clump, and the dip is black. Hydrogen's dip goes black within a few cells.

15
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.600
amount: one clump goes black

16
00:00:47.653 --> 00:00:50.873
Here the gas is too hot to see. So hot that oxygen loses even a sixth electron and hydrogen keeps almost none. O VI and H I take almost no light here.

17
00:00:50.923 --> 00:00:54.923
Where a dip sits means speed. Moving toward us shifts a shadow bluer, like a siren. Cloud A's dip sits left for its speed, not its order.

18
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.600
speed: where a dip sits

19
00:00:59.391 --> 00:01:00.748
Cloud B

20
00:01:02.006 --> 00:01:06.499
Amount sets how much light is taken. This one cell holds a quarter of all the O VI on the beam. Its dip grows term by term: amount, width, place.

21
00:01:07.637 --> 00:01:12.130
Heat spreads it out. Hotter ions jiggle faster, so the same amount spreads over more speeds: wider and shallower.

22
00:01:13.394 --> 00:01:19.058
Change the heat, watch the dip. Warmer gas spreads the dip wider. But O VI lives only in a band of heat: outside it, the dip fades.

23
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.820
ion share: heat decides the ion

24
00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:29.180
Past halfway, new dips still appear.

25
00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.874
New O VI dips appear at +61, +98 km/s. The order of dips is not the order of clouds.

26
00:01:32.924 --> 00:01:35.704
Far apart, same speed, same dip. Gas 84 kpc apart moves at almost the same speed, so the spectrum cannot tell them apart: the clearest of 2 such pairs.

27
00:01:35.754 --> 00:01:38.550
The spectrum keeps speed and loses place. A dip records how fast gas moves along the beam, not where it sits.

28
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.820
place is lost, speed is kept

29
00:01:41.936 --> 00:01:45.463
More gas, deeper dip. More atoms darken a dip's centre while its width hardly changes, until the centre is black.

30
00:01:45.513 --> 00:01:48.550
Once black, only wider. Cloud D's hydrogen lands on a dip that is already black: the trough widens, but cannot get darker.

31
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.600
amount: deeper, then only wider

32
00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:55.342
This is everything the light carried out.

33
00:01:55.763 --> 00:01:58.550
49 cells left a visible O VI shadow. Their shadows merged into a few broad dips.

34
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.820
metals: an ingredient, not the switch

35
00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:08.506
A blurry telescope merges dips. Every telescope smears the spectrum a little. Less sharpness means neighbouring dips blend into one.

36
00:02:08.568 --> 00:02:12.480
Blur turns narrow black dips grey. A narrow black dip comes out grey, as if light got through. The wide hydrogen trough stays black.

37
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.821
Longer exposures beat noise. Twice as clean needs four times as long. The noise pattern stays the same; only its size shrinks.

38
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:22.324
Noise can hide dips, and invent them. At low exposure the smaller dip at +96 km/s drowns, and chance wiggles look like dips. The white line is the truth.

39
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.600
sharpness and noise

40
00:02:29.447 --> 00:02:33.447
We fit a few components. A fit explains the noisy spectrum with a few smooth components, each with an amount, a width and a speed.

41
00:02:34.196 --> 00:02:38.196
Components are not clouds. One component can draw on gas in several places, and clouds are not cells: one clump, many cells across, can feed several components.

42
00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.564
A spectrum tells how much and how fast. …but not where, and one width alone is not a thermometer.

43
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.950
how much and how fast, not where

44
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
How a spectrum is made

